{{-- A comparison, as a table.

     Prose describing who wins where makes a reader hold two columns in their
     head while they read. A table lets them scan the row they care about and
     stop, which is what somebody comparing two tools is actually doing.

     The `verdict` column carries a word rather than a filled progress bar or a
     score out of ten. Those imply a measurement nobody took, and the honest
     answer to most of these rows is "both, differently".

     Rows come from `compare` in the page's own server script. Partials are
     included into the page's scope, so this reads that binding directly:
     passing it as a prop would arrive empty, since stx drops props between
     components in this version. --}}
<div class="overflow-x-auto -mx-6 mt-12 px-6">
  <table class="min-w-[36rem] w-full text-left border-collapse">
    <caption class="sr-only">How ReportsHQ and {{ compareWith }} differ, feature by feature</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr class="border-b border-line">
        <th scope="col" class="pr-4 py-3 font-medium text-subtle text-xs tracking-wide uppercase">What you are comparing</th>
        <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-subtle text-xs tracking-wide uppercase">ReportsHQ</th>
        <th scope="col" class="pl-4 py-3 font-medium text-subtle text-xs tracking-wide uppercase">{{ compareWith }}</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      @foreach (compare as row)
        <tr class="border-b border-line/60 align-top">
          <th scope="row" class="pr-4 py-4 font-medium text-ink text-sm">{{ row.about }}</th>
          <td class="px-4 py-4 leading-relaxed text-muted text-sm">{{ row.ours }}</td>
          <td class="pl-4 py-4 leading-relaxed text-muted text-sm">{{ row.theirs }}</td>
        </tr>
      @endforeach
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
